<Base title="Privacy policy | Starlink Ghana Monitor" description="How the independent Starlink Ghana Monitor extension and calculator handle network readings, account information and device data." path="/privacy/">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 leading-relaxed">
    <a href="/" class="underline underline-offset-4">← Back to Starlink Ghana Monitor</a>
    <h1 class="mt-8 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="mb-6 text-sm opacity-70">Last updated: 10 October 2026</p>
    <p>Starlink Ghana Monitor is an independent open-source tool built on Dishylink. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by Starlink or SpaceX.</p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Local monitoring</h2>
    <p>While Chrome is running on your Starlink network, the extension reads connection performance, energy, outages and available router/device usage information. It saves recorded readings, cost assumptions and preferences in your browser. It does not read the pages you visit. By default, raw minute readings remain in your browser; optional paired sync and a separate opt-in recovery vault are described below. The extension also keeps a rolling recent-minute safety copy in a separate browser extension storage area, to aid recovery after an IndexedDB-only reset. This is still local to the same Chrome profile, not an off-device backup.</p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Optional Starlink account</h2>
    <p>You can monitor your local network without signing in. If you connect your Starlink account, the extension reads your existing Starlink browser session and requests account, plan, billing, location and device information directly from Starlink. Account controls you use can change network settings. It retains a connection preference, not a permanent copy of your login cookies. The session may remain available in your ordinary Starlink browser login.</p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">External connections</h2>
    <p>Optional features can contact Starlink for account functions, CelesTrak for orbital data, Cloudflare for a speed test, our public website for version updates and GitHub for downloads. Hosts may receive standard connection metadata such as your IP address. The website calculator stores your inputs locally. You may choose to connect a separate local collector by providing its address.</p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Optional phone companion sync</h2>
    <p>The public <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/live/">phone companion</a> requires a private pairing link created by the laptop extension. If you opt in, the extension uploads aggregated traffic, energy, cost and recording freshness summaries approximately every ten minutes while Chrome runs. We store a hashed pairing credential and the latest summaries in Supabase PostgreSQL, not Starlink authentication cookies or device MAC addresses. The optional paired snapshot may include up to 16 device display names, groups and monthly router usage shares. These labels could contain personal names. Disconnecting the phone pairing deletes its latest synced phone snapshot. If you also opted into the separate history vault, its older minute records are held in an independent archive and are not automatically deleted when a pairing is revoked. Anyone with your private pairing link can view those summaries. Disconnect from the extension to revoke the link and delete its cloud snapshot.</p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Optional history vault</h2>
    <p>Version 1.6.0 offers a separately enabled cloud backup of individual minute timestamps, sampled power and upload/download volume. Unlike phone summaries, these readings can reveal household activity patterns. Opting in is not required to use the monitor. Archive writes are append-only and authenticated with your existing private pairing capability; archived minutes are stored in the owner's isolated Supabase PostgreSQL table. This is a server-side private archive, not end-to-end encrypted with a separate user password. It does not upload device names, MAC addresses, Starlink credentials or browsing history. The companion phone can download a private backup of archived readings. Keep the file in a second safe location.</p>
    <p>Pausing protection stops future archive uploads but does not erase earlier minutes. Revoking your phone pairing also removes the normal route to read the vault; before revoking, download a local archive backup. Contact the project maintainer to request erasure of already archived data when no longer needed. The cloud archive and browser cache are not a substitute for separate user-controlled backups.</p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Your choices</h2>
    <p>You may decline optional location or account features, disconnect your Starlink account, and export your history and settings. Removing the extension or clearing its storage deletes locally recorded data unless you have exported a backup. Backups may contain sensitive usage patterns, so keep them private.</p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Limited use and support</h2>
    <p>We use Chrome permissions to provide and improve the monitor's features, not for advertising or sale of user data. Use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the Chrome Web Store User Data Policy, including its Limited Use requirements.</p>
    <p>For a detailed explanation, read the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="https://github.com/hayalows/starlink/blob/master/PRIVACY.md">full privacy policy</a>. Contact the project through <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="https://github.com/hayalows/starlink/issues">GitHub Issues</a>.</p>
  </main>
</Base>
